<think>
Core Principle Identification:
- Oracle tagging/direct-sum construction: To combine finitely many sparse sets S1,...,Sk into one sparse oracle S, tag each element x ∈ Si with a constant-size identifier for i to form disjoint copies; define S = ⋃_{i=1}^k {tag(i) ∘ x : x ∈ Si}. Sparsity per length follows by summing polynomial bounds with only constant-length shifts.
- Sparsity preservation under constant-length padding: If |Si^{=m}| ≤ pi(m) for polynomials pi, then for S^{=n} formed by adding a constant overhead ci to lengths, |S^{=n}| ≤ Σ_i pi(n − ci), which is polynomial in n.
- P_bad-angel equals P: If the “angel” advice αn is computable in polynomial time A(n), then a poly-time decider can compute αn and then run M(x, αn). Conversely, any P language fits P_bad-angel by choosing trivial advice αn (e.g., ε) and an M that ignores it. Therefore P_bad-angel = P. Consequently, NP = P_bad-angel iff P = NP.
- Encoding advice into a sparse oracle via padding: Given L ∈ P_angel with polynomial p(n) and angel string αn ∈ {0,1}^{p(n)}, construct a sparse set SL that contains, for each length n, at most p(n) strings of a designated length t(n) encoding the positions j where αn[j] = 1. Choose t(n) to be a strictly increasing polynomial that dominates p(n) (e.g., t(n) = (n+1)^r with r ≥ deg p), so that for length m = t(n), the count is ≤ p(n) ≤ m^c for some constant c (e.g., c = 1 if r ≥ deg p), ensuring sparsity. A poly-time oracle TM can reconstruct αn by querying all j ∈ [1..p(n)] and then simulate the original M(x, αn).
