- Impartial combinatorial games (normal play): Positions are winning (N) or losing (P). A position is losing (P) if and only if no allowed move leads to a P-position; it is winning (N) if and only if at least one allowed move leads to a P-position.
- For the subtract-a-square game, moves are n → n − k^2 where k ≥ 1 and k^2 ≤ n. Hence: n ∈ W iff ∃ k with k^2 ≤ n such that n − k^2 ∈ L; otherwise n ∈ L. Base case: 0 ∈ L.
- Counting argument over initial positions up to N: Let L(N) be the number of losing positions in {0,1,...,N}, W(N) the number of winning positions. Then L(N) + W(N) = N + 1. Each losing l ≤ N can generate at most floor(√(N − l)) ≤ √N winning positions ≤ N of the form l + k^2. Hence W(N) ≤ Σ_{l ∈ L, l ≤ N} floor(√(N − l)) ≤ L(N)√N. Combining yields L(N) ≥ (N + 1)/(√N + 1), which grows unbounded with N, implying infinitely many losing positions.

