3) Show every winning position n ≤ N has a representation n = l + k^2 with l ∈ L, hence W(N) ≤ Σ_{l ≤ N, l ∈ L} number of k with k^2 ≤ N − l = Σ floor(√(N − l)) ≤ L(N)√N.
4) Combine with step 2 to get L(N) ≥ (N + 1)/(√N + 1).
5) Conclude L(N) → ∞ as N → ∞. Therefore there are infinitely many losing positions (P-positions). Since starting in a P-position makes the first player lose (and thus the second player has a winning strategy), there are infinitely many initial situations where the second player can force a win.
