Core Principle Identification:
- Factor modulus: 2021 = 43 × 47 (both primes).
- Multiplicativity and prime-power formula for divisor sums: For a = ∏ p_i^{e_i}, σ(a^n) = ∏ σ(p_i^{n e_i}) with σ(p^t) = 1 + p + … + p^t = (p^{t+1} − 1)/(p − 1).
- Modular cases for a prime modulus q:
  - If p ≡ 0 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ 1 (mod q).
  - If p ≡ 1 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ t + 1 (mod q).
  - If p ≠ 0,1 (mod q), then σ(p^t) ≡ 1 (mod q) ⇔ (p^{t+1} − 1) ≡ (p − 1) (mod q) ⇔ p^t ≡ 1 (mod q).
- Group-theoretic exponent: For prime q, the multiplicative group (Z/qZ)^× is cyclic of order q − 1; requiring x^n ≡ 1 for all x ∈ (Z/qZ)^× forces (q − 1) | n.
- Chinese Remainder Theorem: Congruences mod 43 and mod 47 combine to mod 2021.
  
Pitfall Prediction:
- Forgetting the special case p ≡ 1 (mod q), which forces q | n (since σ(p^{ne}) ≡ ne + 1 (mod q) must equal 1 for all e).
- Ignoring p ≡ 0 (mod q), though it imposes no constraint (σ ≡ 1 automatically).
- Assuming only (q − 1) | n is enough; in fact, both q | n and (q − 1) | n are needed.
- Misapplying σ(p^t) formula or mixing t with n and e (t = n e).
- Miscomputing lcm(42, 46, 43, 47) or the final sum of distinct prime factors.
- Misinterpreting “sum of the prime factors” as counting multiplicity; standard reading is sum of distinct prime divisors.

